//AI-generated clarification on point-in-time reads 
	/*get_at(key, field, ts) treats ts as now. It reads the current state and only uses ts to check whether the current value has expired. Timestamps are monotonic, so there's no "past" to consult; any value that was overwritten or deleted is gone.

	get_at_time(key, field, ts) treats ts as a historical moment, separate from now. It has to answer "what value was visible at that time," even if that value has since been overwritten, deleted, or expired. It's usually specified with two times, get_value_at(key, field, now_ts, target_ts), so the query itself happens at now_ts while asking about target_ts.

	Example:

	t=1  set(a, x, 10)
	t=5  set(a, x, 20)
	t=8  delete(a, x)
	Query	Result
	get_at(a, x, 9)	nothing (deleted)
	get_at_time(a, x, now=9, target=3)	10
	get_at_time(a, x, now=9, target=6)	20
	get_at_time(a, x, now=9, target=8)	nothing (tombstone at 8)

	Storage consequence: you can no longer overwrite in place. Each field becomes a version list of (write_ts, value or tombstone, expiry), sorted by write_ts. A read is then an upper_bound on target_ts, a step back one entry, and checks for tombstone and expiry relative to target_ts.

	Questions this forces you to answer:

	Unbounded memory. Do you keep history forever? If not, you need a retention window or GC horizon, and queries older than it return an error.
	Interaction with restore. Does restore rewrite history, or append new versions at restore time? Appending is the MVCC-consistent answer; rewriting breaks past reads.
	Expiry semantics. A value with TTL that expired at t=7 is invisible at target=7 but visible at target=6. This is where the half-open [ts, ts+ttl) decision starts to matter.
	Scans at a past time. scan_at_time has to resolve every field's version independently, which is O(fields · log versions).
	*/
